        Statistical Independence Aware Caching for LLM Workflows
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Dai%2C+Y">Yihan Dai</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Bouras%2C+D+S">Dimitrios Stamatios Bouras</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Jia%2C+H">Haoxiang Jia</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Mechtaev%2C+S">Sergey Mechtaev</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2511.22118v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> (LLMs) <span class="search-hit mathjax">inference</span> is both expensive and slow. Local caching of responses offers a practical solution to reduce the cost and latency of LLM queries. In research contexts, caching also enhances reproducibility and provides flexibility for experimentation. Howev&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2511.22118v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2511.22118v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2511.22118v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> (LLMs) <span class="search-hit mathjax">inference</span> is both expensive and slow. Local caching of responses offers a practical solution to reduce the cost and latency of LLM queries. In research contexts, caching also enhances reproducibility and provides flexibility for experimentation. However, naive reuse of cached responses compromises statistical independence, a critical property for probabilistic workflows. In applications of LLM for code, it underpins performance metrics such as Pass@k and uncertainty estimation, as well as algorithms like program repair loops and retries. Existing LLM caching systems lack ways to enforce statistical independence constraints. To address this, we introduce Mnimi, a cache design pattern that supports modular LLM workflows while ensuring statistical integrity at the component level. Its core innovation lies in encapsulating statistical constraints within the type of LLM references, allowing users to manage and transform these types according to the scope and requirements of their algorithm. We implemented this design pattern in Python using a combination of decorators and iterators over infinite sequences. A case study on SpecFix, an recent automated program specification repair system, highlights how Mnimi improves reproducibility, ease of debugging, time and cost efficiency while preserving statistical correctness.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2511.22118v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2511.22118v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

